schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Optimism's Retro Funding thesis: \"easier to agree on what was useful than what will be useful\""
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Optimism's Retroactive Public Goods Funding pays projects for public goods
    they shipped in the past rather than for promised future work; the thesis
    (from the 2021 post introducing the idea, with Vitalik Buterin as guest
    author of the key section) is "it's easier to agree on what was useful than
    what will be useful."
  evidence: >-
    Cited to "Optimism, Retroactive Public Goods Funding" (Medium, July 20,
    2021, with Vitalik Buterin as guest author of the core section) in the
    article's References.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/writing-grant-proposals.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used to establish the second of the article's "two kinds of grants"
    distinction — retroactive grants require documenting delivered impact with
    metrics, never aspirational language, which changes what a proposal
    contains.
  uncertainty: >-
    Direct paraphrase/quotation of a named, dated, attributable source (a 2021
    Optimism blog post); low uncertainty on attribution, though the article
    does not link the live OP Atlas Retro Funding program details beyond
    naming atlas.optimism.io/missions as a reference.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/writing-grant-proposals.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
